  "notes": "The entry states his role in one line — \"**he is the harmonic author of the lane**\" — so this model authors chords harder than anything else in the producer batch. The vocabulary is the entry's own: \"m9/m11/13 voicings with the 3rd and 7th in the inner voices, quartal stacks, chromatic passing chords between diatonic targets, gospel-derived plagal and deceptive cadences, and reharmonisation of the same melody on each repeat. Cycles run 4 or 8 bars with real motion, not 2-bar vamps.\" No progression families are authored, deliberately: a fixed family is the opposite of reharmonising the same melody each pass, and the entry names cadence *types* rather than numerals. What is authored is a triad weight of 0.02, the highest ninth share in this batch, `susSubstitutionProb` for the quartal stacks, short chord durations, and a harmonic rhythm that moves every bar rather than vamping. Drums are his own when he produces: \"programmed with a swung 16th grid and a live percussion overlay\", so the kit is moderately quantised with a continuous 16th hat and a dense hand-percussion layer, unlike Questlove's no-click kit. His arrangements \"always leave a mid-register hole for the keyboard answer\", which is the countermelody at −12 with the highest density ratio in this batch — the keyboard answers every vocal phrase. Bass is \"played bass (his own or Palladino's); where a keyboard bass is used it is a Clavinet or Minimoog line rather than an 808\" — no `bass808` block, and the chord voicing starts at 50 to clear a bass that reaches 48. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number and all melody/countermelody figures; the research marks the specific voicing vocabulary as idiom inference from the records.",
